---
description: Independent SDK and connector release rules
alwaysApply: true
---

# Independent Releases

- Each connector is versioned and released on its own. Its manifest
  metadata.version is the release source of truth and is independent of other
  connectors and of the SDK version. An unchanged version explicitly defers
  release.
- A connector PR that should release moves only that connector's version one
  step from its latest tag: patch for a fix, minor for an addition such as an
  operation or mocks, and for a breaking change (breaking) with a minor bump
  before v1 or a major bump from v1. A change that breaks connections, as
  docs/versioning-and-releases.md defines, is a major bump also before v1:
  v0 releases v1.0.0. First releases use v0.1.0. Tooling accepts a skipped
  step or another first version, so review checks both.
- SDK tags use sdkgo/vX.Y.Z; connector Go tags use their `go/` module directory.
- The one-time Go module move uses exactly one minor bump from the latest old-path Go tag, including v1 connectors. The new import path forces opt-in; old tags stay as history.
- Python and TypeScript connector packages use independent `spec.languages.python.version` and `spec.languages.typescript.version` values, with matching package metadata.
- A Python or TypeScript package lists exactly the operations and Triggers it implements in `spec.languages.<language>`; the catalog and site show per-capability language support.
- Every Python and TypeScript connector package must replay Go-verified provider scenarios from `conformance/connectors/<directory>/scenarios.json`. Cover each Go-tested operation branch, Retry, uncertainty, request count, and provider idempotency-key behavior. CI requires fixtures for new or changed Python and TypeScript packages and any connector with existing fixtures. Existing packages without fixtures report `missing conformance (legacy)` on Go-only changes. Porters write scenario inputs, prior state, and provider responses; Go record mode writes complete requests, exact outputs, production idempotency keys, and retry decisions. CI verifies a nonzero value for every input field, each declared branch, separate final-attempt 429 and 5xx Retry scenarios and a transport failure per operation, uncertainty when declared, SDK-encoded exact outputs, and exact Go recordings. Pending scenarios are rejected; other recovery paths remain a review requirement.
- SDK tags also use sdkpython/vX.Y.Z and sdkts/vX.Y.Z; connector language tags append /python/vX.Y.Z or /typescript/vX.Y.Z.
- Release each language SDK before connectors that require it. New npm packages use the CI token for first publish; existing packages use trusted publishing when configured.
- Connector modules depend on an exact published SDK version without replace,
  pseudo-version, branch, or commit SHA.
- Dex Server is backward compatible with every earlier Dex SDK release. Pin no
  Dex Server, Dex Go SDK, or Dex CLI version; record minimums instead. A
  module's go.mod records a minimum github.com/superdurable/dex/sdk-go
  release. Raise it only when the module needs a newer release, then to the
  newest stable release, after reading that release's Breaking Changes section.
- Use only released Dex versions: no prerelease, pseudo-version, or replace.
- CI resolves the latest stable Dex CLI and Dex Go SDK on every run. Set
  DEX_CLI_VERSION only to reproduce a failure.
- A connector may require another connector module only at an exact released
  tag that is reachable from `main` and whose GitHub release has
  `connector-release.complete`. Release the dependency first and pin it in a
  later PR.
- From this repository, connector modules may require only the SDK and other
  connector modules, never the root module or an `examples/` module.
- Release SDK changes before a later connector dependency-upgrade PR.
- When discovering an SDK contract gap, develop SDK and connector changes
  together with the ignored `go.work` from `make workspace` or a temporary
  local `replace` and run the full connector verification. Never commit that
  `replace`. Before handoff, split
  the SDK changes into a preceding SDK PR, merge and release its tag, then pin
  that exact published SDK version from the connector PR.
- Run module verification with GOWORK=off. Connector releases run automatically
  after merge to main; manual runs only repair incomplete releases and catalog
  deployment.
- A Go release candidate <connector directory>/go/vX.Y.Z-rc.N publishes one connector
  from a commit that is not on main through the Release Connector Candidate
  workflow. The commit changes only that connector's directory (a new connector
  also its catalog entry and company logo), and vX.Y.Z is its manifest version,
  after its latest release. A candidate is a GitHub pre-release with the release
  assets, never a release, a public catalog entry or a connector dependency.
- v0 breaking releases cannot be patch releases. v1+ breaking releases require
  a major module-path migration.
- Use one released module per PR unless a repository-wide mechanical change
  requires several. A connector batch port PR may contain 5-8 connectors of one
  language when every package is a first `v0.1.0` release and passes conformance.
  The coordinator merges one batch at a time and confirms its Release run,
  including first publish of new PyPI or npm projects, succeeded before merging
  the next batch because PyPI limits new-project creation rates.
- Use sdkgo:, sdkpython:, sdkts:, connector(<slug>):, connectors(python):,
  connectors(typescript):, tooling:, or docs: subjects.
- Put exact lowercase (breaking) in the PR and final commit message for public
  breaking changes.
